WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.671
<v Examiner>DNA, chromosomes and mutations.

2
00:00:04.121 --> 00:00:07.905
<v Examiner>Describe the structure and function of DNA and RNA.

3
00:00:07.905 --> 00:00:13.619
<v Examiner>Then define some of the ways genetic abnormalities occur, including mutation,

4
00:00:13.619 --> 00:00:15.919
<v Examiner>genetic disease and aneuploidy.

5
00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:18.679
Two checklist points in one track.

6
00:00:18.679 --> 00:00:22.280
Her checklist calls the DNA part a review from B N 1,

7
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:28.123
and her Module 3 quizzes only touch it lightly, so here's a version that answers both.

8
00:00:28.573 --> 00:00:34.738
The hook: DNA is the recipe book, a chromosome is one volume, a gene is one recipe,

9
00:00:34.738 --> 00:00:36.595
and a mutation is a typo.

10
00:00:37.045 --> 00:00:39.034
DNA is deoxyribonucleic acid.

11
00:00:39.034 --> 00:00:43.494
It's a double helix: two strands of nucleotides twisted together,

12
00:00:43.494 --> 00:00:47.337
each nucleotide made of a sugar, a phosphate and a base.

13
00:00:47.337 --> 00:00:53.169
The bases pair up across the middle: adenine with thymine, and guanine with cytosine.

14
00:00:53.169 --> 00:00:58.383
Its function: it stores the genetic code, and it's the molecule of heredity,

15
00:00:58.383 --> 00:01:01.608
passing traits from one generation to the next.

16
00:01:02.058 --> 00:01:06.758
RNA, ribonucleic acid, is single-stranded, with the sugar ribose,

17
00:01:06.758 --> 00:01:08.999
and uracil in place of thymine.

18
00:01:08.999 --> 00:01:15.072
Its job is to carry the code out of the nucleus, as messenger RNA, to the ribosomes,

19
00:01:15.072 --> 00:01:16.807
where proteins are made.

20
00:01:16.807 --> 00:01:21.941
That RNA part is the textbook review; her Module 3 bank doesn't ask it.

21
00:01:23.391 --> 00:01:24.349
Now her words.

22
00:01:24.349 --> 00:01:28.660
A chromosome is a coiled piece of DNA that contains many genes.

23
00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:32.971
A gene is a segment of DNA that codes for a trait or a protein.

24
00:01:32.971 --> 00:01:35.298
Humans have forty-six chromosomes.

25
00:01:35.298 --> 00:01:40.498
A gamete, a sperm or an egg, has twenty-three, and the zygote has forty-six,

26
00:01:40.498 --> 00:01:43.646
half from the mother and half from the father.

27
00:01:43.646 --> 00:01:49.189
And in meiosis, crossing over, where paired chromosomes swap pieces at a chiasma,

28
00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:53.363
can separate genes that sit close together, the linked genes.

29
00:01:53.363 --> 00:01:55.485
That's one source of variation.

30
00:01:55.935 --> 00:01:58.196
How genetic abnormalities happen.

31
00:01:58.196 --> 00:02:02.856
A mutation is a permanent change in the genetic structure of a gene:

32
00:02:02.856 --> 00:02:04.569
a change in the DNA code.

33
00:02:04.569 --> 00:02:07.926
A genetic disease is one caused by a faulty gene.

34
00:02:07.926 --> 00:02:13.477
Her examples: Huntington's, which is dominant, and cystic fibrosis and Tay-Sachs,

35
00:02:13.477 --> 00:02:14.847
which are recessive.

36
00:02:14.847 --> 00:02:20.946
Her quiz also notes that schizophrenia and Down syndrome are not simple recessive traits.

37
00:02:20.946 --> 00:02:24.509
And aneuploidy is an abnormal number of chromosomes,

38
00:02:24.509 --> 00:02:27.181
as in Down syndrome or Turner syndrome.

39
00:02:27.181 --> 00:02:29.031
That one has its own track.

40
00:02:29.481 --> 00:02:31.570
One more, from her true or false:

41
00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:35.241
why does a fatal recessive disease like Tay-Sachs persist?

42
00:02:35.241 --> 00:02:40.873
Because carriers, the heterozygotes, don't have the disease, but they pass the allele on.

43
00:02:41.323 --> 00:02:42.005
<v Examiner>Once more.

44
00:02:42.005 --> 00:02:44.118
<v Examiner>DNA, chromosomes and mutations.

45
00:02:46.568 --> 00:02:51.018
DNA, deoxyribonucleic acid, is a double helix of nucleotides,

46
00:02:51.018 --> 00:02:55.030
with adenine paired to thymine and guanine to cytosine.

47
00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:58.532
It stores the genetic code and passes traits on.

48
00:02:58.532 --> 00:03:05.097
RNA is single-stranded, with ribose and uracil, and carries the code out to make proteins.

49
00:03:05.097 --> 00:03:08.234
A chromosome is coiled DNA with many genes,

50
00:03:08.234 --> 00:03:12.611
and a gene is a segment that codes for a trait or a protein.

51
00:03:12.611 --> 00:03:18.885
A gamete has twenty-three chromosomes and the zygote forty-six, half from each parent.

52
00:03:18.885 --> 00:03:22.824
A mutation is a permanent change in a gene's DNA code.

53
00:03:22.824 --> 00:03:27.347
A genetic disease comes from a faulty gene, like Huntington's,

54
00:03:27.347 --> 00:03:29.462
cystic fibrosis or Tay-Sachs.

55
00:03:29.462 --> 00:03:32.964
Aneuploidy is an abnormal number of chromosomes.
